-- title:
--   Saturation, full rank and products for an order coordinate
-- statement:
--   Fix rationals $a,b$, a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule $\Lambda$ of the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$, and a natural number $q$ carrying a primality instance; write $W =$ `Zp2 q` for the Witt vectors of the field with $q^2$ elements. Let $\mathrm{coord} : \Lambda \to W \times W$ satisfy `IsOrderCoord`, i.e. $\mathrm{coord}$ is additive and injective, sends $1$ to $(1,0)$ whenever $1 \in \Lambda$, satisfies $\mathrm{coord}(mm') = (\alpha\alpha' + q\beta\,\phi(\beta'),\ \alpha\beta' + \beta\,\phi(\alpha'))$ (with $\phi$ the Witt-vector Frobenius) whenever $mm' \in \Lambda$, has image dense in the sense that for every $k$ and every $(\alpha,\beta) \in W\times W$ some $m \in \Lambda$ has both coordinates congruent to $\alpha,\beta$ modulo the ideal $(q^k)$, and sends $m$ with $m + \bar m = n \in \mathbb{Z}$ to a first coordinate of trace $n$. Then four assertions hold simultaneously: (i) if both coordinates of $m \in \Lambda$ lie in the ideal generated by $q^K$, then $m = q^K m'$ for some $m' \in \Lambda$; (ii) every $h \in \mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ has $d\,h \in \Lambda$ for some integer $d > 0$; (iii) for all $m,m' \in \Lambda$ there is $n$ coprime to $q$ with $n\,(mm') \in \Lambda$; (iv) there is $n$ coprime to $q$ with $n \in \Lambda$ and $\mathrm{coord}(n) = (n,0)$.
--
--   These are the lattice-theoretic facts — saturation of $\Lambda$ at $q$, $\Lambda \otimes \mathbb{Q} = \mathbb{H}$, and closure of the localisation $\Lambda_{(q)}$ under multiplication with the expected coordinates on rational integers prime to $q$ — which let statements normally proved for an order $\mathcal{O}$ with $\mathcal{O} \otimes \mathbb{Z}_q$ a maximal order in the quaternion division algebra be used for a bare submodule equipped with a coordinate map. They feed the comparison of the formal module attached to such a $\Lambda$ with the special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module, and the analysis of torsion points on the associated fake elliptic curve.
